mptoms, hypercalcemia, and elevated alkaline phosphatase (suggesting bone turnover) strongly supports malignancy-related hypercalcemia. These findings are typical of cancer with bone metastases or paraneoplastic syndromes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset and constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fatigue) argue against vitamin D toxicity as the primary hypercalcemic mechanism?", "summary": "Subacute presentation is less typical of acute vitamin D toxicity, which usually has rapid onset. This reduces but does not exclude vitamin D/granulomatous hypercalcemia as a potential cause."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented PTH level or bone resorption markers reduce confidence in parathyroid-related hypercalcemia as the diagnosis?", "summary": "Lack of PTH testing weakens evidence for parathyroid-related hypercalcemia. Primary hyperparathyroidism typically has elevated PTH, but this cannot be confirmed without lab data."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented tumor mass/foci in the presentation and normal physical exam weaken the likelihood of malignancy as the hypercalcemia cause?", "summary": "While the physical exam is unremarkable, the presence of constitutional symptoms and elevated alkaline phosphatase maintains moderate suspicion for malignancy, as early cancer/tumor-associated hypercalcemia may lack obvious mass findings."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of granulomatous disease features (e.g., hilar lymphadenopathy, sarcoid-like symptoms) in the presentation disfavor vitamin D/granulomatous hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Absence of granulomatous disease features (sarcoidosis, lymphomas) reduces support for vitamin D/granulomatous etiology, but doesn't exclude it entirely as granulomatous disease can present without these signs."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal physical e